@database "datetime"
@author "Enrico Ehrich"
@$VER: datetime.guide 1.0 (30.09.2026)
@smartwrap

@node main "Date and Time"
@{h1}Date and Time

A clock and a calendar for agents. Language models do not know what time it
is and are unreliable at date arithmetic; this plugin gives an agent one
tool that answers both: the current time in any timezone, adding and
subtracting durations, converting between timezones and to Unix
timestamps, weekdays, calendar facts, business days and days until a date.
It asks the clock when the agent calls it, so the time reaches the model as
a fresh answer instead of being frozen into the prompt. It keeps no state
and has no panel.

@{code}
For users
  @{"  Setting it up  " link setup}

For agents and developers
  @{"  The datetime tool  " link tool}
  @{"  Operations in detail  " link operations}
  @{"  What the model sees  " link model}
@{body}
@endnode

@node setup "Setting it up"
@{h1}Setting it up

The plugin is on in the default configuration (@{tt}config/plugins.yaml@{utt}):

@{code yaml}
plugins:
  servers:
    datetime:
      type: datetime
      enabled: true
@{body}

An agent sees the tool when its allowlist names it:

@{code yaml}
agent_config:
  tools:
    allowed:
      - "+datetime/*"
@{body}

It needs @{tt}pytz@{utt}, which is part of the core requirements. The
plugin reads nothing from its server entry. Its only setting is the
system-wide @{tt}context.timezone@{utt} in @{tt}config/config.yaml@{utt},
the zone of "now" when a call names none (see
@{" The datetime tool " link tool}). It stores nothing on disk or in memory
between calls.
@endnode

@node tool "The datetime tool"
@{h1}The datetime tool

One tool, named after the instance plus @{tt}_operations@{utt}:
@{tt}datetime_operations@{utt} in the default setup. The
@{tt}operation@{utt} parameter picks what it does.

@{h2}Operations

@{table}
Operation | Does
current | the current date and time
format | reads a date and writes it in another format
parse | reads a date and breaks it into its parts
add_time, subtract_time | a date plus or minus a duration; add and subtract are accepted too
convert_timezone | the same moment in another timezone
to_timestamp | a date as a Unix timestamp; timestamp is accepted too
calendar_info | weekday, week of year, quarter, leap year, days in the month and the month as a small calendar
business_days | business days between two dates, or a date plus N business days
day_of_week | the weekday of a date
days_until | calendar days from one date to another
@{body}

@{h2}Parameters

These are the ones the model is offered. Nothing has a default the model
must know: what is left out is taken as below.

@{table}
Parameter | Used by | Meaning
operation | all | required; an unknown name answers "Unknown operation: x"
datetime | all but current | the input date; left out: now (see timezone). format and parse require it
timezone | see below | an IANA name like Europe/Berlin, or UTC, or local (the server's clock, without offset); default below
format | current, format | iso, human, date_only, time_only, or a strftime pattern; default iso for current, %Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S for format
input_format | format | a strptime pattern for datetime instead of the automatic reading
years, months, days, hours, minutes, seconds | add_time, subtract_time | the duration; each 0 when left out
days | business_days | business days to add (negative: go back)
target_date | days_until, business_days | the end date
@{body}

What @{tt}timezone@{utt} does depends on the operation:

@{table}
Operation | timezone is
current | the zone the answer is given in
add_time, subtract_time, calendar_info, day_of_week, days_until, business_days | the zone of "now" when no datetime is given; ignored when one is
convert_timezone | the target zone
format, parse, to_timestamp | ignored
@{body}

Without @{tt}timezone@{utt}, "now" is in @{tt}context.timezone@{utt} from
@{tt}config/config.yaml@{utt} (Europe/Berlin by default) -- the zone the
system prompt's current date is rendered in, so the tool's "today" is the
prompt's today, also around midnight. When that setting is missing or not a
known zone, it is UTC. convert_timezone does not use it: its target without
@{tt}timezone@{utt} is UTC.

A @{tt}format@{utt} that is not one of the four names is used as a strftime
pattern as it stands: a word without @{tt}%@{utt}, like "short", comes back
literally.

@{h2}How dates are read

@{bullet}Most operations take ISO 8601: @{tt}2026-01-15@{utt},
@{tt}2026-01-15T14:30@{utt}, @{tt}2026-01-15T14:30:00+01:00@{utt}; a
trailing @{tt}Z@{utt} means UTC. Anything else is refused with Python's
text, e.g. "Invalid isoformat string: 'next friday'".
@{bullet}@{tt}format@{utt} and @{tt}parse@{utt} read more: a plain number is
a Unix timestamp in UTC (so "20260115" is 23 August 1970), and besides ISO
8601 a few written forms -- @{tt}15.01.2026@{utt}, @{tt}15/01/2026@{utt};
parse also @{tt}15.01.2026 14:30@{utt}, @{tt}January 15, 2026@{utt},
@{tt}15 January 2026@{utt} and @{tt}Thursday, January 15, 2026@{utt}. With
slashes the day comes first: @{tt}03/04/2026@{utt} is the 3rd of April;
@{tt}01/15/2026@{utt} is read month first only because 15 cannot be a month.
@{bullet}A date without offset stays without one -- except for Unix
timestamps, where it counts as UTC, and in convert_timezone, where it is
taken as UTC.

@{h2}Answers

Every answer has @{tt}status@{utt}, @{tt}success@{utt} or
@{tt}error@{utt}. The fields of each operation are in
@{" Operations in detail " link operations}. A failure answers
@{tt}{"status": "error", "error": "..."}@{utt}, mostly with the input
echoed under @{tt}input@{utt}. The plugin's own texts:

@{table}
error | When
Missing required parameter: operation | no operation
Unknown operation: x | a name not in the list above
Invalid timezone: x | an unknown zone in timezone
Missing required parameter: datetime | format without datetime
Missing required parameter: datetime_string or datetime | parse without datetime
Could not parse datetime: x | format or parse could not read the date
business_days needs target_date (to count) or days (to add) | business_days with neither
Missing required parameter: target_date | days_until without it
@{body}

Anything else -- a date that is not ISO 8601, an input_format that does not
match ("time data '15.01.2026' does not match format '%Y'") -- answers
the same way with Python's text.

@{h2}An example

@{code json}
{"operation": "current", "timezone": "Europe/Berlin", "format": "human"}
{"operation": "add_time", "datetime": "2026-01-31", "months": 1}
{"operation": "convert_timezone", "datetime": "2026-01-15T14:30:00Z", "timezone": "America/New_York"}
{"operation": "business_days", "datetime": "2026-09-01", "target_date": "2026-09-10"}
@{body}

The second answers @{tt}2026-02-28T00:00:00@{utt}, the third
@{tt}2026-01-15T09:30:00-05:00@{utt}, the fourth 7.
@endnode

@node operations "Operations in detail"
@{h1}Operations in detail

@{h2}current

The fields: @{tt}current_time@{utt} (ISO 8601 with offset),
@{tt}timestamp@{utt} (the same ISO text -- not a number),
@{tt}unix_timestamp@{utt} (the number, whole seconds), @{tt}timezone@{utt}
(the zone's name, "Local" for local), @{tt}year@{utt} ... @{tt}second@{utt},
@{tt}weekday@{utt} (0 is Monday), @{tt}weekday_name@{utt},
@{tt}month_name@{utt} and @{tt}formatted@{utt} in the requested format.
human looks like "Wednesday, September 30, 2026 at 04:22:45 AM".

@{h2}format and parse

@{tt}format@{utt} answers @{tt}original@{utt}, @{tt}formatted_time@{utt},
@{tt}parsed_datetime@{utt} (ISO), the parts year to second,
@{tt}weekday@{utt}, @{tt}weekday_name@{utt} and @{tt}month_name@{utt}.

@{tt}parse@{utt} answers @{tt}original@{utt}, @{tt}parsed_format@{utt} (the
pattern that matched, @{tt}unix_timestamp@{utt} for a number,
@{tt}iso8601@{utt} for other ISO forms), @{tt}parsed_datetime@{utt} and
@{tt}iso_format@{utt} (the same), @{tt}unix_timestamp@{utt} and
@{tt}components@{utt}: the parts plus @{tt}quarter@{utt},
@{tt}day_of_year@{utt} and @{tt}week_of_year@{utt} (ISO week). It is the
way to turn a Unix timestamp into a date.

@{h2}add_time and subtract_time

Weeks and days are added first, then months and years; a day that the
target month does not have becomes its last day: 31 January plus one month
is 28 February, and 31 March minus one month is 28 February too. These
calendar units move the wall clock. Hours, minutes and seconds come last
and are elapsed time.

Without @{tt}datetime@{utt} the start is now in @{tt}timezone@{utt} (default:
the prompt's zone, see @{" The datetime tool " link tool}), and the two
kinds of units differ across a daylight-saving change. In Berlin, now plus
60 days in September keeps the hour and ends in +01:00; 01:30 on 25 October
plus 2 hours is 02:30+01:00, and 01:30 on 29 March plus 1 hour is
03:30+02:00. A given datetime carries a fixed offset, and the result keeps
it. The answer: @{tt}original@{utt} (the input, or
"current time"), @{tt}result@{utt} and @{tt}result_datetime@{utt} (the
same ISO text), @{tt}human_readable@{utt}, and the duration under
@{tt}added@{utt} or @{tt}subtracted@{utt}.

@{h2}convert_timezone

A date without offset is taken as UTC. The answer:
@{tt}original_time@{utt} and @{tt}converted_time@{utt} (ISO),
@{tt}human_readable@{utt} with the zone's abbreviation,
@{tt}time_difference_hours@{utt} (the target's offset minus the source's),
@{tt}from_timezone@{utt} and @{tt}to_timezone@{utt}. Without
@{tt}timezone@{utt} the target is UTC. Without @{tt}datetime@{utt} it
converts now.

@{h2}to_timestamp

@{tt}timestamp@{utt} (whole seconds), @{tt}timestamp_milliseconds@{utt},
@{tt}iso_format@{utt} and the input under @{tt}datetime@{utt}. A date
without offset is UTC; without @{tt}datetime@{utt} it is now.

@{h2}calendar_info

@{tt}date@{utt}, year, month, @{tt}month_name@{utt},
@{tt}month_abbr@{utt}, day, @{tt}weekday@{utt} (0 is Monday),
@{tt}weekday_name@{utt}, @{tt}weekday_abbr@{utt}, @{tt}day_of_year@{utt},
@{tt}week_of_year@{utt} (ISO week), @{tt}quarter@{utt},
@{tt}is_weekend@{utt}, @{tt}is_leap_year@{utt}, @{tt}days_in_month@{utt},
@{tt}first_weekday_of_month@{utt} (0 is Monday) and
@{tt}calendar_month@{utt}, the month as a small text calendar.

@{h2}business_days

Business days are Monday to Friday; holidays are not known.

@{bullet}With @{tt}target_date@{utt}: counts the business days from
@{tt}datetime@{utt} (default today) up to the target -- the first day
counts, the target does not. The answer: @{tt}business_days_between@{utt},
@{tt}total_days@{utt}, and @{tt}dates_swapped@{utt} true when the target
lay before the start (the count is the same either way).
@{bullet}With @{tt}days@{utt}: steps that many business days forward
(backward when negative), skipping Saturdays and Sundays. The answer:
@{tt}result_date@{utt}, @{tt}weekday@{utt}, @{tt}weekday_name@{utt},
@{tt}is_business_day@{utt}. With 0 it is the start date, weekend or not.
@{bullet}With both, target_date wins: it counts.

Times are dropped; only the dates count.

@{h2}day_of_week and days_until

@{tt}day_of_week@{utt} answers @{tt}day_of_week@{utt} (the name),
@{tt}weekday_number@{utt} (0 is Monday) and @{tt}is_weekend@{utt}.

@{tt}days_until@{utt} counts calendar days from @{tt}datetime@{utt} (default
today in @{tt}timezone@{utt}) to @{tt}target_date@{utt}, dates only:
@{tt}days@{utt} (negative when the target has passed),
@{tt}is_future@{utt}, @{tt}is_past@{utt}, @{tt}is_today@{utt}.

@{h2}For direct callers

The code also reads parameters the model is not offered: @{tt}weeks@{utt}
for add and subtract, @{tt}from_timezone@{utt} and @{tt}to_timezone@{utt}
for convert_timezone (to_timezone wins over timezone), @{tt}start_date@{utt},
@{tt}end_date@{utt} and @{tt}add_business_days@{utt} for business_days and
days_until (they win over datetime, target_date and days),
@{tt}timestamp@{utt} for to_timestamp (the other direction: a date from a
Unix timestamp), @{tt}year@{utt} and @{tt}month@{utt} for calendar_info,
and @{tt}format: custom@{utt} with @{tt}custom_format@{utt} for current.
@endnode

@node model "What the model sees"
@{h1}What the model sees

@{h2}The tool

The description and parameters come from the plugin's
@{tt}schema.yaml@{utt}; they are short on purpose, since they are sent with
every request. The model sees the parameters of
@{" The datetime tool " link tool}, not the ones for direct callers. Error
texts are listed there.

@{h2}The status line

While it works: "Processing current datetime operation". When done, the
operation and its answer, cut at 70 characters: "current:
2026-09-30T04:22:45.393738+02:00", "days_until: 85", "calendar_info: Wednesday",
"business_days: 7", "add_time: Wednesday, September 30, 2026 at 06:13:03
AM". A failure shows "day_of_week failed: Invalid timezone: Mars/Olympus".

@{h2}Tokens and cache

The time reaches the model only as a tool answer, appended to the end of
the conversation like any other. The plugin has no hook and adds nothing
to the system prompt, so the cached prompt in front of it stays intact.
That is the point of asking a tool: a clock written into the system prompt
or near the start of the history changes on every turn and makes the whole
conversation behind it miss the cache. Prompt templates may therefore use
@{tt}current_date@{utt} (it changes once a day) but not the time; a test
(@{tt}tests/config/test_prompts_have_no_ticking_clock.py@{utt}) holds them
to that. An agent that needs the exact time calls this tool.

An answer is a few hundred characters; calendar_info carries the month as a
text calendar as well.

@{h2}Known gaps

@{bullet}No holidays: business_days knows only weekends.
@{bullet}format and parse read a plain number as a Unix timestamp, and
slash dates day first.
@{bullet}timezone is ignored for a given datetime: 14:30 without offset
stays 14:30 without offset. To place a local time in a zone, write the
offset into the date (@{tt}2026-01-15T14:30:00+01:00@{utt}).
@{bullet}Durations are not checked: a value that is not a number answers
with Python's error text.
@endnode
